## What the program measures (from `ising.py`)

Metropolis, checkerboard updates, random (hot) start per (T, seed), `--burn` sweeps discarded, every sweep measured. Per run: means of |m|, m², m⁴, e, e² (per site); χ = L²(⟨m²⟩−⟨|m|⟩²)/T (the "connected" |m| susceptibility, peaks above T_c at finite L); C = L²(⟨e²⟩−⟨e⟩²)/T²; U = 1 − ⟨m⁴⟩/(3⟨m²⟩²). With `--series DIR` it saves per-sweep |m| and e as `L{L}-T{T:.4f}-s{seed}-{m,e}.npy`.

## Known physics to compare against (textbook, not measured here)

- Exact T_c = 2/ln(1+√2) = 2.269185 (Onsager).
- Binder cumulant at T_c for the square-lattice periodic Ising model: U* ≈ 0.6107 (Salas & Sokal 2000; Kamieniarz & Blöte 1993). Binder crossings converge to T_c with corrections ~L^(−1/ν−ω), small in 2D.
- χ peak (of the |m| form) at finite L lies above T_c, shifting as T_peak(L) − T_c ∝ L^(−1/ν), ν = 1; for L=64 it is expected near 2.30, so a 0.1 grid gives the peak at 2.3 most likely.
- Metropolis dynamic exponent z ≈ 2.17; τ_int(|m|) near T_c grows strongly with L — L=128 (and 256 with only 10,000 sweeps) is where 20,000 sweeps may be marginal.
- Low-T random starts can trap in domain/stripe states at large L (slow coarsening), which can bias U below T_c; worth checking in the series.

## Not established / decisions taken

- Burn-in for stages 2 and 3 is not stated: we use the program default 2,000 (as in stage 1). Stage 3 says "10,000 sweeps" = measured sweeps.
- "Bootstrap over seeds" with 3 seeds gives few distinct resamples (10 multisets); we report that limitation and add a block-bootstrap within the time series as a robustness check.
- Peak location method: the grid maximum (as specified), with a local quadratic fit as a refinement reported alongside; the stage-2 grid is centred on the grid maximum unless otherwise noted.
